1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,683
You are driving down a busy street

2
00:00:02,953 --> 00:00:05,833
and you finally spot a gap
between two parked cars.

3
00:00:06,273 --> 00:00:09,483
The road is wide, so you have
plenty of room to swing out,

4
00:00:09,713 --> 00:00:13,283
but the space itself
looks uncomfortably tight.

5
00:00:14,153 --> 00:00:16,883
Nobody enjoys shuffling back and forth,

6
00:00:17,113 --> 00:00:20,533
inching closer to the curb
a few centimeters at a time.

7
00:00:20,933 --> 00:00:24,433
You want to reverse into the space
in one smooth sweep

8
00:00:24,753 --> 00:00:26,233
and pull straight forward to park.

9
00:00:26,253 --> 00:00:28,933
So here is the mathematical question:

10
00:00:29,393 --> 00:00:32,533
How much extra length,
beyond the length of your own car,

11
00:00:32,813 --> 00:00:34,733
does that parking space actually need?

12
00:00:35,933 --> 00:00:39,033
In 2009, mathematician Simon Blackburn

13
00:00:39,033 --> 00:00:43,683
formulated a precise model for what
he called a perfect parking maneuver.

14
00:00:44,313 --> 00:00:48,283
First, there is no handbrake skidding
and no multi-point shuffling.

15
00:00:48,573 --> 00:00:53,133
You reverse into the space at full
steering lock in a single continuous arc,

16
00:00:53,413 --> 00:00:56,683
stopping the exact moment
your car is parallel to the curb.

17
00:00:57,113 --> 00:00:59,093
Then, you straighten your front wheels

18
00:00:59,093 --> 00:01:02,183
and drive straight forward
into the middle of the space.

19
00:01:02,713 --> 00:01:04,633
To find the minimum space required,

20
00:01:04,973 --> 00:01:08,433
we only need to analyze the geometry
of that reversing sweep.

21
00:01:10,533 --> 00:01:13,383
To turn a real car into clean geometry,

22
00:01:14,073 --> 00:01:18,633
imagine slicing the vehicle horizontally
right through the centers of its four wheels,

23
00:01:18,993 --> 00:01:20,683
looking straight down from above.

24
00:01:21,193 --> 00:01:24,283
The extreme outer body
forms a simple rectangle,

25
00:01:24,653 --> 00:01:26,933
labeled A, B, C, D.

26
00:01:27,393 --> 00:01:31,883
The curb runs along the straight line
through A, E, F, and D.

27
00:01:32,173 --> 00:01:35,783
Or, if you prefer never to scrape
your alloy wheels on concrete,

28
00:01:36,093 --> 00:01:39,573
an imaginary line a few centimeters
to the left of the curb.

29
00:01:39,573 --> 00:01:43,933
Notice that our curbside wheels,
centered at points E and F,

30
00:01:44,253 --> 00:01:47,483
line up closely with the right edge
A to D of the car.

31
00:01:49,133 --> 00:01:52,033
How does a car actually rotate
when reversing?

32
00:01:52,573 --> 00:01:55,733
Its rear wheels are fixed parallel
to the sides of the car.

33
00:01:56,113 --> 00:02:00,053
That means the imaginary line passing
through both rear wheel centers

34
00:02:00,053 --> 00:02:04,783
must always point directly at the center
of the turning circle, labeled X.

35
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:06,633
What about the front wheels?

36
00:02:07,093 --> 00:02:11,413
Because the inside front wheel travels along
a tighter circle than the outside wheel,

37
00:02:11,693 --> 00:02:15,233
it has to steer at a sharper angle
to avoid dragging sideways.

38
00:02:15,793 --> 00:02:17,493
This is called Ackermann steering —

39
00:02:17,493 --> 00:02:21,133
patented back in 1818
for horse-drawn carriages.

40
00:02:21,573 --> 00:02:24,733
When all wheel axle lines
meet at a single point X,

41
00:02:25,013 --> 00:02:27,933
every part of the car
turns cleanly around X.

42
00:02:29,733 --> 00:02:34,083
Blackburn chose four measurements for his formula
that you can easily look up on the web,

43
00:02:34,493 --> 00:02:36,433
or measure yourself with a tape measure.

44
00:02:36,873 --> 00:02:40,373
First is r — the radius of your car's
curb-to-curb turning circle,

45
00:02:40,373 --> 00:02:45,383
which is the distance from the front
wheel E to the turning center X.

46
00:02:45,933 --> 00:02:50,983
Second is l — the wheelbase
between the front wheel E and rear wheel F.

47
00:02:51,473 --> 00:02:57,073
Third is k — the front overhang from the
front wheel E to the front bumper corner A.

48
00:02:57,073 --> 00:03:02,733
And fourth is w — the width GH
of the parked car in front of you.

49
00:03:03,193 --> 00:03:06,183
Our goal is to find
the extra distance AH.

50
00:03:08,333 --> 00:03:13,083
Before we dive into the proof,
here is the exact formula we are going to build.

51
00:03:13,633 --> 00:03:17,013
At first glance, nested square roots
might look intimidating,

52
00:03:17,353 --> 00:03:22,513
but every single piece inside this expression
comes directly from Pythagoras' theorem

53
00:03:22,513 --> 00:03:25,083
applied to three right-angled triangles.

54
00:03:25,793 --> 00:03:30,083
Let's freeze the parking maneuver
at its most critical instant and see why.

55
00:03:32,933 --> 00:03:37,683
Look directly down from above, just as
we finish reversing parallel to the curb.

56
00:03:38,293 --> 00:03:44,483
As the car reverses around center X, the wheels
at E and F travel along the two dashed circles.

57
00:03:44,913 --> 00:03:49,213
Meanwhile, the front-right corner A
of our car sweeps along the outer circle.

58
00:03:49,633 --> 00:03:51,853
In the tightest possible parking space,

59
00:03:52,033 --> 00:03:56,333
that corner A will just barely miss
the corner G of the car parked in front.

60
00:03:56,913 --> 00:04:00,973
Because corner A passes right through
point G along that outer circle,

61
00:04:00,973 --> 00:04:05,533
both A and G lie at the exact same
distance from the center X.

62
00:04:07,533 --> 00:04:12,083
Step 1 of our proof focuses
on the right-angled triangle EFX.

63
00:04:12,593 --> 00:04:16,173
Because the rear axle line goes straight
through the turning center X

64
00:04:16,173 --> 00:04:20,933
at a right angle to the side of the car,
the angle at F is 90 degrees.

65
00:04:21,573 --> 00:04:25,233
We know the hypotenuse EX
is the turning radius r,

66
00:04:25,373 --> 00:04:28,883
and the vertical side EF
is the wheelbase l.

67
00:04:29,513 --> 00:04:33,593
Applying Pythagoras' theorem gives us
the horizontal distance FX —

68
00:04:33,593 --> 00:04:36,933
the square root of r² minus l².

69
00:04:39,133 --> 00:04:44,213
For step two, look at the taller
right-angled triangle AFX.

70
00:04:44,693 --> 00:04:50,233
Its vertical side AF is simply
the wheelbase l plus the front overhang k,

71
00:04:50,613 --> 00:04:53,433
and we just found the base FX in step one.

72
00:04:53,893 --> 00:04:56,133
Using Pythagoras' theorem a second time

73
00:04:56,133 --> 00:05:00,233
gives us the hypotenuse AX,
the radius of the outer circle.

74
00:05:00,713 --> 00:05:03,183
And that leads immediately to step three.

75
00:05:03,633 --> 00:05:06,413
Since point G lies on that exact same circle,

76
00:05:06,413 --> 00:05:09,583
the distance GX is identical to AX.

77
00:05:11,733 --> 00:05:16,583
Now for step four — the key geometric
construction shown in Figure 3.

78
00:05:17,073 --> 00:05:21,553
We draw a new line straight down
from corner G, parallel to the curb,

79
00:05:21,753 --> 00:05:26,083
until it meets the horizontal line FX
at a new point, K.

80
00:05:26,593 --> 00:05:30,573
In step five, notice that the distance
FK at the bottom

81
00:05:30,573 --> 00:05:34,083
is equal to the width w
of the parked car at the top.

82
00:05:34,553 --> 00:05:37,233
Since we already know the full length FX,

83
00:05:37,233 --> 00:05:41,783
the remaining segment KX is simply FX minus w.

84
00:05:44,333 --> 00:05:48,433
In step six, we apply Pythagoras'
theorem one last time,

85
00:05:48,713 --> 00:05:51,583
to the right-angled triangle GKX.

86
00:05:51,993 --> 00:05:57,933
We know the hypotenuse GX from step three,
and we know the base KX from step five.

87
00:05:58,393 --> 00:06:03,283
Subtracting their squares and taking the
square root gives us the vertical height GK.

88
00:06:03,773 --> 00:06:05,433
Finally, step seven.

89
00:06:05,773 --> 00:06:11,173
The vertical height GK is the exact same
length as HF along the curb.

90
00:06:11,173 --> 00:06:14,883
Subtract the wheelbase l
and front overhang k,

91
00:06:15,233 --> 00:06:19,533
and what remains is AH —
the exact extra space we need.

92
00:06:21,933 --> 00:06:24,583
Let's put some realistic numbers
into the formula.

93
00:06:25,253 --> 00:06:29,833
Suppose your car has a curb-to-curb
turning circle diameter of 10.8 meters,

94
00:06:30,393 --> 00:06:33,383
which means the radius r is 5.4 meters.

95
00:06:34,053 --> 00:06:40,053
Let's say the wheelbase l is 2.6 meters,
the front overhang k is 1.3 meters,

96
00:06:40,333 --> 00:06:44,433
and the parked car in front
has a width w of 1.7 meters.

97
00:06:44,913 --> 00:06:49,933
When we crunch those numbers, the formula
tells us the parking space must be at least

98
00:06:49,933 --> 00:06:53,283
the length of your car plus 1.43 meters.

99
00:06:55,533 --> 00:06:59,733
Looking closely at the formula,
you might notice something surprising:

100
00:07:00,513 --> 00:07:03,533
It depends on the width w
of the parked car in front,

101
00:07:03,913 --> 00:07:06,833
but it does not include
the width of your own car at all.

102
00:07:07,293 --> 00:07:07,783
Why?

103
00:07:08,193 --> 00:07:11,133
Because in a single-sweep maneuver
on a wide road,

104
00:07:11,353 --> 00:07:15,983
the critical pinch point is your curbside
front corner A clearing point G.

105
00:07:16,393 --> 00:07:20,713
Making your car wider pushes
edge BC closer to the center X,

106
00:07:20,993 --> 00:07:22,893
without changing the path of corner A.

107
00:07:22,893 --> 00:07:27,583
In real life, of course, a wider car
does make a difference on narrow roads,

108
00:07:27,833 --> 00:07:29,683
or when you have to shuffle back and forth.

109
00:07:32,133 --> 00:07:34,933
What if you parallel park
using a different method —

110
00:07:35,273 --> 00:07:37,583
pointing your car toward
the curb at an angle,

111
00:07:37,753 --> 00:07:41,883
reversing straight back, and putting on
full lock at the very last minute?

112
00:07:42,453 --> 00:07:45,633
Blackburn's geometry still tells you
the best angle of approach.

113
00:07:46,053 --> 00:07:49,683
You apply full lock right as
corner A passes point G,

114
00:07:49,973 --> 00:07:52,983
placing your car onto the
exact same turning circle.

115
00:07:53,413 --> 00:07:58,153
The ideal angle of approach is simply
the angle a tangent to the circle at G

116
00:07:58,153 --> 00:08:01,483
makes with the curb,
minus the tangent angle at A.

117
00:08:03,733 --> 00:08:08,013
What if the extra space c
is smaller than 1.43 meters

118
00:08:08,013 --> 00:08:10,833
and you don't mind shuttling back and forth?

119
00:08:11,413 --> 00:08:15,413
Mathematically, as long as the space
is even slightly longer than your car,

120
00:08:15,673 --> 00:08:18,183
you can always park
if you use enough movements.

121
00:08:18,693 --> 00:08:21,583
By tracking the midpoint
between your two rear wheels,

122
00:08:21,913 --> 00:08:26,083
each forward or backward movement
follows two opposite circular arcs,

123
00:08:26,373 --> 00:08:29,983
moving you closer to the curb
by a distance of twice PA —

124
00:08:30,133 --> 00:08:32,313
though you always have to finish by reversing

125
00:08:32,313 --> 00:08:34,933
so your front bumper never swings
over the pavement.

126
00:08:37,333 --> 00:08:41,893
Finally, if you park nose-first
into a multi-story car park bay

127
00:08:41,893 --> 00:08:44,883
while steering your front corner B
along a straight line,

128
00:08:45,293 --> 00:08:49,283
your rear corner C traces a classic
curve called a tractrix,

129
00:08:49,493 --> 00:08:53,233
proving that wherever you park,
geometry is in the driver's seat.
